-- kernel_laws.lth: the laws the kernel's structures satisfy, and the roles of the core's `data`.
--
-- Draft 0, 2026-10-13, by Claude Opus 5.5 in the session (step 2.1 of
-- record/plan-from-zero-2026-10-13-execution.md). Sources: spec 3.S7, 3.S8, 3.S36, 3.S37, 4.R8;
-- D-2026-10-12-ak (an object is a type with its equality, whose laws the kernel guarantees for a
-- structural `==`); D-2026-10-13-t (the roles of the core's `data`: `Unit` terminal, `Empty`
-- initial, `Bool` a Boolean algebra, `Nat` its laws, `List` the free monoid); L2.7 (every
-- constructor has a law of its role; everything has laws, found in the place the thing holds in a
-- known structure). The v0.1 file is at the tag v0.1-final, src/luthier/lib/kernel_laws.lut.
--
-- What this file states. Each section is a known structure as a theory, in the pattern of
-- ebnf.lth: a record type of its operations, its laws over a model `(m : T)`, and `T := laws`.
-- The core's types are models of them, which gives each constructor its role: `unit` is the point
-- of a terminal object, `true` and `false` the top and bottom of a Boolean algebra, `nil` the
-- neutral element of a free monoid. Those models are values, so they go to kernel_models.lut
-- (spec 12.S23), not here; the last section lists them. Equality is the structural `==` of the
-- kernel (4.R8), which §1 states as an object.
--
-- Names: `and`, `or`, `not` and `mod` are operators or core names, so fields are named by the
-- structure's own words (`meet`, `join`, `complement`); `unit` is a constructor of core, so a
-- monoid's element is `neutral`.
--
-- Index. Status of each section: done, or todo with what it needs. A session or Codex continuing
-- this file takes the first todo, writes it in the pattern of the done ones, checks it with
-- `luthier parse src/luthier/lib/kernel_laws.lth`, sets its status to done here, and commits.
--
--   §1 Object: a type with its equality; reflexive, symmetric, transitive,
--      substitutive (D-2026-10-12-ak, 3.S36).                                   todo
--   §2 Terminal and initial objects: `Unit`, `Empty` (3.S7, 3.S8, D-2026-10-13-t).    todo
--   §3 Boolean algebra: `Bool`'s `true`, `false`, with `not`, `and`, `or`
--      (D-2026-10-13-t); Huntington's laws.                                    todo
--   §4 Monoid and free monoid: `List`'s `nil`, `cons`, `append`; the recursion
--      principle `FoldNil`, `FoldCons` (3.S36, D-2026-10-13-t).                 todo
--   §5 Commutative semiring with its order: `Nat`'s laws (11.R1, D-2026-10-13-t).    todo
--   §6 The constructions of the kernel: `Beta`, `PairFst`, `PairSnd`, `Pairing`,
--      `IfTrue`, `IfFalse`, `CaseNothing`, `CaseJust` (3.S36); what is not statable
--      (eta, 3.S37).                                                            todo
--   §7 The models, to write in kernel_models.lut: `==` of each concrete type as an
--      `Object`; `Unit` a `Terminal`, `Empty` an `Initial`; `Bool` a `BooleanAlgebra`;
--      `List A` a `FreeMonoid A`; `Nat` a `CommutativeSemiring`.                 todo
